POTENTIAL EDGE CASES
================================================================================

1. Missing secrets in existing .env
   Scenario: .env exists but ARMORCLAW_KEYSTORE_SECRET is missing
   Behavior: Installer generates new KEYSTORE_SECRET, preserves existing ADMIN_TOKEN and MATRIX_SECRET
   Impact: Keystore.db encrypted with old secret will be inaccessible
   Recommendation: Document that secrets must exist in .env for successful migration

2. Corrupted secrets in existing .env
   Scenario: Secrets contain invalid characters or wrong format
   Behavior: Installer will preserve them as-is (grep doesn't validate)
   Impact: Unknown - depends on how services handle invalid secrets
   Recommendation: Add secret validation in migration logic

3. Partial .env file
   Scenario: .env exists but is incomplete or malformed
   Behavior: Installer will read what it can, generate missing secrets
   Impact: May succeed with mixed preserved/generated secrets
   Recommendation: Validate .env structure before migration

4. Multiple re-runs
   Scenario: User runs installer multiple times with same domain
   Behavior: Each run preserves existing secrets (no-op on secrets)
   Impact: Safe - secrets remain unchanged across runs
   Status: ✅ This is the correct behavior

5. Mode downgrade (sentinel → native)
   Scenario: User runs installer with no domain on sentinel installation
   Behavior: MODE changes to native, secrets preserved, sentinel variables removed
   Impact: Safe - data preserved, but sentinel-specific config lost
   Status: ✅ This is the correct behavior

